Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  07 REMOVED_MONTH 94 ACCESSIONED ON  07 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 16 39 CLINICAL DATA  Status post biopsy right breast mass                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     LYMPH NODES  RIGHT AXILLARY   EXCISION  METASTATIC BREAST CARCINOMA IN ONE OF TWELVE LYMPH NODES  BREAST  RIGHT   LUMPECTOMY  1  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  THREE FOCI  NOTE  Ductal carcinoma in situ is present at one inked lateral margin  2  INTRADUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  3  BIOPY SITES WITH FIBROSIS  CHRONIC INFLAMMATION  FAT NECROSIS AND FOREIGN BODY GIANT CELL REACTION  BREAST  RIGHT   BASE OF BIOPSY  BREAST TISSUE  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  Diagnosis by  Austin L  Vickery  M D  Signed On  13 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 14 23                                                                                 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in three separately labeled parts  Labeled  Re excision   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm lumpectomy specimen with a REMOVED_DATE x 0 9cm skin ellipse at one edge  Three healing scars  REMOVED_DATE  REMOVED_DATE  and 1 3cm in length are present parallel to each other on the skin and they contain multiple stitches  The specimen is inked black  In addition the deeper aspect is inked yellow  Sectioning the specimen transversely reveals two irregular  tan yellow  gelatinous areas  One is 1 5cm in greatest dimension and 0 5cm beneath the skin  The other one is 0 6cm in greatest dimension  4 0cm beneath the skin ellipse  and 0 4cm from the deep margin and contains old blood clot  In addition a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm relatively firmer  white tan  fibrous satelite area is present in between the two previous biopsy sites  The remaining breast parenchyma is unremarkable and mostly composed of soft  yellow  adipose tissue  Representative sections from the breast parenchyma  and biopsy sites and all the firmer areas are submitted in A through M  Labeled  Axillary nodes   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm piece of fibroadipose tissue with attached scant skeletal muscle  Multiple lymph nodes ranging in size from REMOVED_DATE to 1 3cm are dissected from the adipose tissue  Only one of the lymph nodes has a focally white and firm cut surface  The other lymph nodes are grossly unremarkable  Representative half each lymph node are submitted in N through Q  the firmer lymph node in N   Labeled    base of biopsy   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm piece of fibroadipose tissue  Sectioning reveals grossly unremarkable adipose tissue with attached scant  fibrous tissue at one edge  representative two thirds of the specimen including all of the firmer areas are submitted in R through T  Resident  Metin Ozdemirli  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Edwin Lee Carter  M D  Jay Solomon Markowitz  M D  PART A  LYMPH NODES  RIGHT AXILLARY   EXCISION PART B  BREAST  RIGHT   RE EXCISION PART C  BREAST  RIGHT  BASE  BIOPSY   report_end  , Not_reported.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  24 MAY 96 ACCESSIONED ON  24 MAY 96 at 12 41 CLINICAL DATA  73 year old woman with a left breast mass found on screening mammogram                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY  1 INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  REMOVED_DATE CM  WELL DIFFERENTIATED   GRADE I III   FOCALLY PRESENT LESS THAN REMOVED_DATE CM FROM AN INKED SURFACE   SEE NOTE  NOTE  No definite blood vessel or lymphatic vesselinvasion is identified  The frozen section gross impression is confirmed  2 DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  SOLID AND CRIBRIFORM PATTERNS  LOW GRADE   ASSOCIATED WITH AND EXTENDING AWAY FROM THE INVASIVE COMPONENT  AND FOCALLY LESS THAN REMOVED_DATE CM FROM AN INKED SURFACE  3 FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES  Diagnosis by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  29 MAY 96 at 12 35                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  11 REMOVED_MONTH 96 at 13 59 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number only  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 2cm fragment of breast with a localizing wire in place and an accompanying x ray  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a 0 9cm  white  stellate firm nodule located adjacent to an inked resection margin  All fibrous tissue is submitted in A through J  with the suspicious nodule in A through D  remaining non  suspicious fatty tissue   1 0 x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm   is not submitted  No other masses are seen    Resident  Douglas B  Flieder  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS  LESION SUSPICIOUS FOR INVASIVE CARCINOMA BUT TOO SMALL TO FREEZE  O R  Resident  EDI BROGI  MD  PhD O R  Diagnosis by  Gunnlaugur P  Nielsen  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  David William Rattner  M D  Diane Elise Rich  M D  PART   BREAST TISSUE  LEFT    report_end  , No.
